Mute the transmitter when the DAC stops being fed

patches/0004 mutes the transmitter from the buffer's postdisable hook, and
describes that as a real guarantee on the grounds that the IIO core runs
postdisable even when the application crashed or was killed.

It measured false. tools/IDLE-CASES.md, case B: kill -9 a LOCAL process
feeding a TX buffer and buffer/enable stays 1, so the IIO core never runs
postdisable, the mute never fires, and the transmitter stays live
indefinitely. Through a 20 dB loop the port read -46.7 dBFS against -59.3
dBFS muted - 12.6 dB hotter, with both the writer and its feeding process
confirmed gone and nobody having asked for anything. Case C, a client that
stalls mid-stream without dying, was never covered at all.

tools/tx-guard.sh mitigates this from userspace and says plainly why that is
not enough: it is a tool-level gate rather than enforcement, anything writing
out_voltageN_hardwaregain directly bypasses it, and its affirmation file lives
in world-writable tmpfs where any process can forge it with touch.

The fix is to stop keying off events. Closing, killing and crashing are
events, and an event can be missed. "No DMA block has arrived for N
milliseconds" is a state, and a state cannot be. One timer closes both B
and C.

  - dds_buffer_submit_block() re-arms it on every block, which is the only
    place the driver reliably learns that data is still flowing.
  - preenable arms it too, so a buffer enabled and then never fed at all -
    which is case C's worst version - is covered rather than sitting unmuted
    with nothing to arm the watchdog.
  - postdisable cancels it, so the clean path is unchanged.

CYCLIC streams are deliberately exempt. The hardware repeats a single
submitted block forever, so silence on the submission path is the feature
working, not a fault, and muting those would break every cyclic transmit on
the board. The flag is read BEFORE the PL DDR FIFO branch, which clears it.

It is a workqueue rather than a timer because muting talks to the AD9361 over
SPI, and that sleeps.

The timeout is tunable and readable at

    /sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceN/tx_starve_timeout_ms

defaulting to 250 ms, with 0 to disable it. Values under 20 ms are refused:
this board's network path already delivers in bursts with gaps, and a timeout
inside that would mute a healthy stream and present as a mysterious dropout.

CYCLIC streams get a SEPARATE, opt-in backstop at

    /sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:deviceN/tx_cyclic_timeout_ms

defaulting to 0, which is off. Outliving the program that started it is what
a cyclic transmit is FOR - docs/block-design.md calls that out as the point of
the DMA's CYCLIC 1 - so nothing changes unless an operator asks. But once
started, a kill -9 is indistinguishable from a normal return, and the board
will happily radiate a repeating waveform forever with nobody watching. Being
able to bound that is worth one attribute.

The stale claim in cf_axi_dds_buffer_stream.c is corrected in place, since a
comment asserting a guarantee that does not hold is worse than no comment.

Measured on the board, all through attenuation read-back rather than trusting
a call to have returned:

  A  normal close            -89.75 dB, buffer 0     unchanged
  B  kill -9, local          -89.75 dB after 0.27 s  was -40 dB, live forever
  C  client stalls alive     -89.75 dB               was never covered
  D  cyclic, running          -40 dB, buffer 1       correctly left alone
  D' cyclic + backstop 2 s   -89.75 dB after 2.10 s  opt-in
  E  timeout 0                -40 dB                 disabled as asked
  F  timeout 5 ms            refused, stays 250      floor enforced

One trap worth recording for anyone writing a test here: do NOT use `sleep`
immediately after `kill -9` on a background job in busybox sh. It returns
instantly on SIGCHLD, so the script reads the attenuation about 10 ms after
the kill and sees the pre-mute value. That nearly went down as "mutes after
3 to 6 seconds" when the real figure is 0.27 s. Poll /proc/uptime instead.

